Slabgrass

Ingredients: BLUEGRASS + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Double Bass + 1980s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: The genre honors a pirate Appalachian radio station that only powered up when lightning struck its grounding mast.

Slabgrass sounds like a bluegrass breakdown dragged through a decommissioned Cold War missile bunker during a cloudburst: furiously fast, finger-picked five-string banjos and doghouse upright basses are slammed through blown-out 1980s digital delay units, gated Soviet-clone plate reverbs, and weaponized industrial drum samplers.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 135–170 BPM. Hyperkinetic, freight-train Appalachian "drive" colliding with rigid, unquantized 1980s drum-machine stomp and violent off-beat slap-back bass clicks.
- Harmonic Palette: High-lonesome modal mountain scales (Mixolydian and Dorian) welded to grim, monolithic minor pentatonics, abrasive power-drone fifths, and the metallic dissonance of ungrounded voltage.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Gated Thunder-Pluck: Banjo and fiddle transients gated hard through an AMS RMX16 reverb unit set to non-linear decay, snapping shut like an iron trapdoor.
  2. Gut-to-Tape Clipping: Upright double basses miked with vintage boundary mics taped directly to raw concrete floors, driven until the 1/2-inch magnetic tape sags.
  3. Cloudburst Modulation: Low-frequency broadcast hum and genuine storm static cross-modulated against the acoustic instruments using 1983-era FM carrier filters.
- Dynamics: Abrupt tectonic shifts. Tracks drop without warning from full-tilt, four-finger barnburner picking into pitch-black industrial silence, broken only by the subterranean rumble of a struck bass and thunder rumbles recorded via telephone lines.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the summer of 1984, along the razor ridges of West Virginia, pirate station W-STRK broadcast exclusively from an abandoned brutalist flood-control tower whenever barometric pressure plummeted below 29 inches of mercury. Founded by out-of-work coal machinists, disaffected mountain string players, and rogue telecom repairmen, the station drew its transmitter power from lightning arrested across an array of eight copper lightning rods. Mountain kids tuned in through AM transistor radios wrapped in wet tin foil to hear blistering breakneck fiddle tunes grafted onto the mechanical brutality of early E-mu Emulator samplers and concrete-shattering double bass slapping. Gatherings were impromptu and feral: spectators parked Ford pickup trucks in horseshoe formations inside abandoned limestone quarries during severe thunderstorm warnings, pounding tire irons against tailgates in sync with the sky-sparked transmissions. Fashion was utilitarian anti-glam: grease-blackened denim jackets lined with copper mesh, Soviet military-surplus stormcoats, steel-toed work boots, and radios dangling from baling wire like talismans.

Central Instruments: 
- The Doghouse Upright Double Bass (strung with weed-whacker nylon and heavy steel gut, battered percussively against the fingerboard and mic’d through a cracked Boss HM-2 Heavy Metal pedal).
- 5-String Deering Banjo retrofitted with magnetic bar pickups.
- Resonator Slide Guitar played with lead-pipe sleeves.
- Oberheim DMX drum machine (battered, loaded with samples of slamming steel silo hatches).
- Yamaha DX7 synthesizer (custom-programmed with glassy, ice-storm bell tones and brutal low-frequency drone squares).

For Fans Of: Bad Livers, The Dillinger Escape Plan (unplugged), Swans (Holy Money era), 16 Horsepower, early Einstürzende Neubauten.

