Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: "Who Touched the Copper Rope?"
- Story & Context: When storm sirens howled across Pike County in August 1984, the neighborhood kids would sprint through pasture mud to grab the radio station’s grounding braid just to feel their teeth rattle before the tape started rolling. Uncle Jessup taped the gut strings of his upright bass directly to the tower’s primary copper arrestor wire while hail tore the tin roof off the shed, recording the take in a single frenzied pass. The track captured both the terrified whoops of children playing lightning-roulette in the gully and the catastrophic pop of the transmitter transformer exploding on beat four. "Turn the dial until your gums bleed red, boy, and don't you dare drop that wire."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Stark 1980s documentary photograph of a child in a mud-splattered yellow rain slicker reaching toward a glowing copper cable anchored into a massive brutalist concrete monolith, distant lightning branching across black clouds, water splashing over rocks, high-contrast monochrome with zinc-blue highlights.

Track 2: Iron Marbles Swallowing Creek Beds
- Story & Context: Corva Vance sat beneath the concrete bypass on Clinch Mountain with her skirt tied in knots, weeping sulfurous tears while feeding ball bearings down the drainage chutes to see which one could outrun the flash flood. Between swigs of warm moonshine, she recounted to the tape machine how she concealed the station’s illicit 200-watt transmitter tube inside her dead hound's doghouse to dodge the county sheriff’s radio sweeps during a July gale. Her confession was backed solely by a frantic double-bass slap that mirrored the clatter of heavy steel balls drowning in the culvert. "I gave the sky my best six-penny nail and buried what was left of us in the limestone."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Surreal album artwork of heavy rusted iron spheres tumbling down a brutally angular concrete water flume into a roaring creek bed, overlaid with the ghosted image of a weeping Appalachian woman holding an illuminated 1980s vacuum tube, grainy analog textures, bruised purple and rust-brown color palette.

Track 3: Sister Hallowell’s Concrete Silo
- Story & Context: Legend says that on the final night of the pirate broadcast, Sister Hallowell refused to evacuate Black Fork Ridge even after seven simultaneous bolts fused her broadcast deck’s pinch rollers into a smoking lump of amber slag. The aftermath was discovered at dawn: the transmitter tower was pulverized, yet the radio in her unlocked station wagon remained stuck looping a ghostly two-finger banjo riff directly from the dead clouds. Searchers found nothing inside the scorched silo except wet chalk marks scoring the walls in counts of four and the smell of boiled transformer oil. "Nothing stays buried when the clouds catch fire, not even the names you scratched in the gravel."
